From 41bbca4d6c7eb274e610139c10d2c7270a94c153 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jason Perlow <jperlow@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 6 Jul 2026 10:19:34 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] drm/panthor: explicitly enable core clock before hw_init (NCZ
 0092)

Confirmed on real Sky1 (.66) hardware tonight, past the 0086 gpu_core
clock-lookup fix: panthor now finds its core clock by name, but its
reported rate is 0 -- clk_prepare_enable() was never actually called
before panthor_hw_init() touches GPU MMIO, causing an asynchronous
SError panic (panthor_hw_init -> panthor_device_init ->
platform_probe).

Root cause: pm_runtime_resume_and_get() right before panthor_hw_init()
is supposed to trigger the runtime_resume callback that calls
clk_prepare_enable on the core/stacks/coregroup clocks. But
__pm_runtime_resume() is gated on this platform (NCZ 0089/0090,
forward-ported from the 7.1.2 investigation) until a late_initcall
sets cix_system_ready -- a fix for OTHER drivers whose premature
deferred-probe resume touches not-yet-clocked hardware. That gate
silently no-ops a device own FIRST, synchronous, in-probe resume too,
so the resume callback that would enable the clock never actually
runs during panthor own probe.

This exact interaction never surfaced during the 7.1.2 investigation
because panthor never got its clock lookup working there either --
0086 (tonight) and 0089/0090 (forward-ported from 7.1.2) individually
worked as designed, but together expose a genuinely new incompatibility.

Fix: explicitly clk_prepare_enable() the core clock right after
panthor_clk_init() succeeds, independent of the pm_runtime path.
clk_prepare_enable is refcounted, so this does not conflict with the
resume callback also enabling it later once cix_system_ready flips true.
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/panthor/panthor_device.c | 20 ++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 20 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/panthor/panthor_device.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/panthor/panthor_device.c
index ea33b6366a7e..9b8909047874 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/panthor/panthor_device.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/panthor/panthor_device.c
@@ -312,6 +312,26 @@ int panthor_device_init(struct panthor_device *ptdev)
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
 
+	/*
+	 * NCZ CIX Sky1 fix: explicitly enable the core clock here rather than
+	 * relying on pm_runtime_resume_and_get() below to do it via the
+	 * runtime_resume callback. On this platform, __pm_runtime_resume() is
+	 * gated (NCZ patches 0089/0090) until a late_initcall marks the system
+	 * ready, to stop OTHER drivers' premature deferred-probe resumes from
+	 * touching not-yet-clocked hardware. But that gate also silently
+	 * no-ops THIS device's own first (synchronous, in-probe) resume, so
+	 * panthor_hw_init() below was running against an unprepared/disabled
+	 * core clock (reported rate=0) and immediately hit an SError touching
+	 * GPU MMIO. Confirmed on real Sky1 (.66) hardware. clk_prepare_enable
+	 * is refcounted, so this does not conflict with the later resume path
+	 * once cix_system_ready flips true and genuinely runs.
+	 */
+	if (!IS_ERR_OR_NULL(ptdev->clks.core)) {
+		ret = clk_prepare_enable(ptdev->clks.core);
+		if (ret)
+			return ret;
+	}
+
 	ret = panthor_pm_domain_init(ptdev);
 	if (ret) {
 		drm_err(&ptdev->base, "init pm-domains failed, ret=%d", ret);
-- 
2.51.0

